ich is being covered of  course by c-span  and we hope this will further public  interest in both the book and the issue  it addresses  the vietnam war obviously is of more  than historical interest as many of the  lessons being drawn from that conflict  today for places like iran and iraq and  afghanistan and perhaps iran you never  can tell  are being drawn from vietnam and in many  cases i would submit are based upon  misinformation and misunderstanding  about what really happened in vietnam  i i've been amused over the years at  listening to experts talk about the  lessons that we should draw from vietnam  and  one of them always is you never go to  war without public support and you never  go to war without the approval in  support of congress of course  in the months surrounding the gulf of  tonkin incident and the enactment of  statutory authorization for war  lbj's public approval rating shot up 58  percent  a full 30 point increase  congress in passing a statute that  jay william fulbright said was  authorizing the president to go to war  did so by a 99.6 percent vote  unanimous in the house and 88 to 2 in  the senate uh i would submit that  neither one of those particular lessons  is is very valid  uh there are lots of lessons about the  vietnam war being an unwinnable war we  should have understood that  i'm not alone in believing that the  vietnam war was not only eminently  winnable but that in fact we had it won  before congress threw in the towel i  worked in the senate during the last  five years of the war or last few years  of the war sorry i worked there five  years but not the war ended during that  time and i think i learned more about  why we lost the vietnam war as a senate  staff member than i did in my two tours  in vietnam  george santana warned us that those who  fail to learn from history are doomed to  repeat it i think we need to take some  take that seriously and i think we can  turn to mark to help us understand what  really happened 