unteers are asked to to go  with a rocket launcher to  to put that out and he volunteers  although he's never  used one of these weapons before and he  goes  uh with charles williams who was also  decorated for this  um they go run through exposing  themselves to fire carry out the mission  he's hit on the way back  shields is and he'll die later that day  and he receives a medal of honor for  this  the u.s navy names a frigate after him  but he's still not really someone we  know  uh again this problem unfortunately we  see a lot of today there's a lot of  heroism on the part of us in iraq now if  you go on the internet look up navy  crossed or distinguished service cross  for example you'll see plenty of  examples  but we don't see  that in our press which uh  and i think certainly is an audience for  it but um i think it has to do with the  attitudes of our of our press in this  country unfortunately  uh in conclusion i'll just  reiterate that vietnam was not in fact  an act of  either hubris or strategic buffoonery  that  in fact it was based on relatively sound  understanding of the  international situation  and this of course again is  very relevant to our times because  vietnam remains  a central issue in our history that that  people base current decisions on  so it's not something we should be  hanging our heads about uh with the  exception of uh the few people who are  involved in cm's overthrow like albert  sam and sheehan  and uh  state department people who are involved  although most of them are now  deceased and as i mentioned to the to a  lesser extent the decision not to go  into north vietnam or laos in 65 is also  another big mistake we make  but  certainly the american deaths in that  conflict were not in vain or in service  of some  lost cause because we do  ultimately achieve our aims of saving  southeast asia from communism  had the united states not gone into  vietnam in 1965. it's almost certain  that the other dominoes of the area  would fall in indonesia  thailand malaysia  for example countries that are of great  importance to us today and that  certainly would have had major  ramifications for the cold war and in  fact would have a lot of ramifications  for our current competition with china  today  veterans day as you know is uh is  approaching  and i think in that spirit we should  thank the  american and south vietnamese veterans  both those who are  still walking amongst us and those who  are not  for protecting the united states and  asia  at this time and performing  great deeds that will continue to adorn  the tapestry of the vietnam war both for  the united states and vietnam  thank you very much  [Applause]  as someone who has taught seminars on  vietnam for undergraduates graduates  military officers over a number of years  i can only say i hope this book is  widely adopted and is read by the next  generation of americans because an awful  lot of our current leaders  our current opinion molders  misunderstand what happened and mark has  presented just a superb summary that  that really  fits with my own experience in vietnam  and studying vietnam here he mentioned  note in zm and i recently had an  encounter with someone who was talking  about i know what it was it was vietnam  magazine had a column referring to  washington's monster note nzm or  something like that and it really i was  not you know i was talking to uh bob  sorley the author of a better war last  he was lecturing in my seminar on  wednesday and  he shared my outrage over that  i had a conversation in 19 december  nineteen sixty eight with dr tron von  doe who had been the state of vietnam's  uh senior representative at the geneva  conference in  june july may june july of 1954 and he  had later uh broken with note in cm but  he said this was a very great patriot a  very able man and a and a  fine leader  more interestingly in 1971 i was driving  back from  quinoa what  from ben tray from what we call quinoa  province and what the viet cong call ben  tray province where they claim the the  revolution began  in 1960  ignoring the fact that hanoi has now  admitted that may 19 1959  the party in hanoi made a decision to  open the ho chi minh trail and liberate  south vietnam this whole issue of the  nlf being autonomous and so forth  doesn't survive the admissions that have  come out of hanoi but  driving back to saigon  late one evening in 1971 i was  accompanied by a man named  who was i think probably the most senior  or most important defector at least that  i encountered in my five  trips to vietnam between 1968 and the  final evacuation in 1975.  he had been chief of culture education  propaganda and training for ben trey  province and was a very senior player  and i asked him just to pass the time  what did you think of zm  and he said when we heard zm had been  killed we thought it must be a trick  because we could not believe the  americans would be so stupid as to allow  anything to happen to zm  he said as a senior party leader  i like other senior party leaders viewed  siam as a great patriot in the same  category as ho chi minh but because he  would not accept the party's leadership  we had to discredit him and so he  denounced him as an american puppet as a  as corrupt as a tyrant as this or that  but it was very clear he had the highest  respect for his um and  that was the experience i also found  that in doing my i did my honors thesis  on the war back in 66  uh and in uh reading the french  histories of vietnam consistently the  french scholars uh it's not consistently  but most of the best french scholars had  praised for zm even though he had  repeatedly refused to serve in there as  a puppet in their governments he had  been asked to head their government time  and again  he would not be their puppet he would  not be japan's puppet when the japanese  took over during world war ii and it  turned out he would not be an american  puppet either when you go through the  pentagon papers one of the interesting  things or the frustration of the  american diplomats who can't understand  why unlike so many third world leaders  this guy was not going to be an american  puppet he was not going to be the  just take orders from the pro council  of the united states in in saigon and  he uh  in fact i remember one of the big fights  that comes out of the pentagon papers  was the imwa said i'm going to face a  guerrilla war i need to have a military  design to fight guerrilla wars  and the americans said no no you're  going to face a korean war 38th parallel  invasion across the 17th parallel you  need a large american style army and zm  said no i don't and they said if you  want money for it that's the army we're  going to buy you and then  of course we stuck them with that army  and then when congress cut off  all the ammunition and funds and pol and  supplies it didn't function very well in  the final months anyway  i've said way too much i'm going to turn  over the  microphone back to mark for questions  and i always like to see if anybody's  really angry give them the first shot i  shouldn't do that to mark but if anybody  really wants to get in a different  viewpoint  raise your hand or something like that  that sounds good i'll leave it here but  donna can you catch it better not do  that she probably would can  raise your hand and god will bring you  over here on this side  or right here  mark i found your comments on president  diem fascinating and i look forward to  reading uh that part of the book  my take on them has always been  not that  he was insufficiently democratic or  not that he was  too autocratic  but  primarily that he was inflexible  that he refused to modernize  that he was fairly incompetent as a  ruler and he just didn't know how to  govern now i assume you're going to  knock all those arguments down  in the book and i look forward to  reading them but give us a preview  yes i do i address those in considerable  length  part of the you mentioned the  stubbornness aspect and he certainly  does get criticized for being stubborn  and  starts in 1955 where there's this  controversy between him versus these  armed religious sects  which some ways a little bit similar to  what we have in iraq right now but  the us government is telling him  you need to compromise with these sects  here  if you don't there's going to be civil  war  and ziem says  i don't think that's the way to go we  cannot allow there to be an independent  armed force within the country  and uh the americans keep telling him  and he keeps saying no  and uh  and he turns out to be right because he  puts down the sex  and that part of the reason why in the  future he won't listen to the americans  very much because they were clearly  wrong  on that  uh on that issue  another thing too with the sam and a lot  of the other vietnamese is they tend to  become more stubborn when the americans  try to tell them what to do  and  so  the ambassadors who are effective  particularly nolting or if you look at  someone like lansdale they attempt to  use persuasion rather than telling them  dictating to them  when cm is dictated to you he does he is  somewhat uh  stubborn as far as  his effectiveness as a leader  again this is where the communist  sources prove critical because  the  a lot of people have tried to say the  6263 period it's not really going that  well for zm but there's account after  account after count from the north  vietnamese side um and from the u.s side  and from the press who later on comes up  with this idea that things are going bad  to talk about how vigorous the  government is in  the strategic hamlet program building up  the militia forces  and just in general prosecuting  the war i mean mekong delta it's a  little more problematic but even there  it's it's uh  it's not in very bad shape so  that in nutshell would uh  summarize you know my answer to that  question  dr moyer i really enjoyed your book i i  had a question you mentioned uh and of  course anyone who's read the book knows  you the word iraq as you said is not  mentioned in your book  but uh you really weave a fascinating uh  story as you talk about the political  turmoil that exists in vietnam in the  time period you look at particularly the  misperceptions that the westerners have  about how politics and power works in  east asia um is there a corollary do you  believe uh with what's going on in iraq  today  uh yes certainly we've been in iraq  especially early on we were assuming  that we could apply our solutions our  western democratic forms of government  and that that was going to fly and  clearly that hasn't been the case um  we're learning  we're getting better on that but there's  still i think probably a ways to go  there  with respect to  the current situation with  the  iraqi prime minister who's now  fairly reluctant to accept our evidence  and we've been critical about how he's  dealing with the conflict there  i think there's a couple important  points there i mean one is that  the government in iraq right now does  not seem to be a nationalist government  which um i think is what is needed ziem  was effective because  he was a nationalist who did not try to  put the interests of one sect ahead of  another  the iraqi government now clearly seems  to be in favor of shia  forces  he's in in close contact with these  death squads and he's trying to to some  extent protect them against the uh  the us um and clearly i think you see  you've seen since the fall of hussein  some of the problems you have in vietnam  when you attempt liberalization i mean  it opens the door  for  subversion  leads to weak government  there's religious violence and so  i think americans are slowly realizing  that we can't  apply our model necessarily to there  as far as  iraqi  turning the iraqi government around we  put ourselves in a hole by uh through  the insistence on democracy because we  now it doesn't seem like we can easily  get  a nationalist leader in there who is  going to  try to to control the shia militia and  disarm them which is again you know  clearly when he disarmed the militias  that was a big boost to his power and  success and whether that's going to  happen now is  remains to be seen we have  somewhat similar situation in early 65  you've got the quad government which is  a  fairly weak government that's  subservient it to the militant buddhists  and  ultimately the military becomes so fed  up with him that  they get together in a big meeting where  they criticize when he finally agrees to  turn the country over to  essentially a military dictatorship  and  that will lead to return of stability  that military is a nationalist uh  predominantly nationalist and whether  there is a nationalist force like that  in iraq now is uh  is hard to say and through you know  debatification we got rid of a lot of  the the people who might have been able  to  to uh  accomplish that i mean we uh you know  clearly  a lot of people are revisiting this  issue of whether democracy was the best  way to go at least um  up front because  i mean a successful democracy doesn't  seem likely to come out of the  the chaos we're seeing there now and  you know ziem argued that  in a time of civil warfare you couldn't  have  um true democracy you had to first bring  stability and then maybe over the longer  term you could  could adopt a somewhat more uh liberal  form of government although he never  really wanted a true democracy but um  you know you see even in our civil war  lincoln certainly does some liberal  things and clamps down on  on opposition so  um  i think that's an issue we're gonna be  confronting seriously in the next few  months  we have time i think for one more  question we've  dr moyer you mentioned um  having access to the communist documents  you mentioned real briefly about china  being concerned about another korean war  did you find any evidence that china or  even the former soviet union were about  ready to withdraw their level of support  or involvement for vietnam  well there's a um  there's not talk of  withdrawing at this time but there there  is a lot of concern  um that they don't want to encourage the  vietnamese too much so the china does go  along with this invasion sort of very  reluctantly but they at the same time  are given the u.s signal i mean they  explicitly state in january 65 that  they're not going to you know fight the  us if the u.s doesn't attack them so  they i mean they can't afford to give up  completely now that particularly  interesting is the soviet union at this  time  uh  going through a transition in their  relations with north vietnam and up  until khrushchev's fall in 64 they've  been pretty standoffish but  new leadership comes in  they  for one thing the north vietnamese need  soviet assistance for anti-aircraft  weapons because the chinese don't have  it  but the the soviets also see this as a  way to get back at the chinese and uh  intrude into the chinese sphere because  by this time they're pretty they're on  pretty bitter terms with each other so  you see a ramp up in soviet support and  this creates a lot of this exacerbates  the tension between china and the soviet  union uh and also the tension between  north vietnam and it's two major patrons  um so  you know a lot of people thought the u.s  and going into vietnam would  bring the communists together but in  fact it it helps to divide them  well we are  out of time our  friends from c-span have  accommodated us for an hour so thanks  very much to them and uh we've got a  reception outside do you have any funnel  thank you very much for coming read the  book and tell your friends because it's  a very important contribution  mark moyer is an associate professor at  the u.s marine corps university in  quantico virginia for more information  about the book or the author mark moyer  visit triumphforsaken.com  [Music]
 

